g.uk/news/blogs/t1-settlement-are-firms-ready-2027 \"Share this selection on Facebook\")\n- [LinkedIn](https://www.fca.org.uk/news/blogs/t1-settlement-are-firms-ready-2027 \"Share this selection on LinkedIn\")\n- [Share this selection by email](https://www.fca.org.uk/news/blogs/t1-settlement-are-firms-ready-2027 \"Share this selection by email\")\n\nShare this selection\n\n- [Tweet](https://www.fca.org.uk/news/blogs/t1-settlement-are-firms-ready-2027 \"Share this selection on Twitter\")\n- [Facebook](https://www.fca.org.uk/news/blogs/t1-settlement-are-firms-ready-2027 \"Share this selection on Facebook\")\n- [LinkedIn](https://www.fca.org.uk/news/blogs/t1-settlement-are-firms-ready-2027 \"Share this selection on LinkedIn\")\n- [Share this selection by email](https://www.fca.org.uk/news/blogs/t1-settlement-are-firms-ready-2027 \"Share this selection by email\")\n\n- [Tweet](https://www.fca.org.uk/news/blogs/t1-settlement-are-firms-ready-2027 \"Share this selection on Twitter\")\n- [Facebook](https://www.fca.org.uk/news/blogs/t1-settlement-are-firms-ready-2027 \"Share this selection on Facebook\")\n- [LinkedIn](https://www.fca.org.uk/news/blogs/t1-settlement-are-firms-ready-2027 \"Share this selection on LinkedIn\")\n- [Share this selection by email](https://www.fca.org.uk/news/blogs/t1-settlement-are-firms-ready-2027 \"Share this selection by email\")\n\nShare this selection\n\n- [Tweet](https://www.fca.org.uk/news/blogs/t1-settlement-are-firms-ready-2027 \"Share this selection on Twitter\")\n- [Facebook](https://www.fca.org.uk/news/blogs/t1-settlement-are-firms-ready-2027 \"Share this selection on Facebook\")\n- [LinkedIn](https://www.fca.org.uk/news/blogs/t1-settlement-are-firms-ready-2027 \"Share this selection on LinkedIn\")\n- [Share this selection by email](https://www.fca.org.uk/news/blogs/t1-settlement-are-firms-ready-2027 \"Share this selection by email\")","metadata":{"url":"https://www.fca.org.uk/news/blogs/t1-settlement-are-firms-ready-2027","ogUrl":"https://www.fca.org.uk/news/blogs/t1-settlement-are-firms-ready-2027","title":"T+1 Settlement: are firms ready for 2027? | FCA","og:url":"https://www.fca.org.uk/news/blogs/t1-settlement-are-firms-ready-2027","favicon":"https://www.fca.org.uk/themes/custom/fca/favicon.ico","og:type":"article","ogImage":"https://www.fca.org.uk/sites/default/files/media/images/fca_social_share.jpg","ogTitle":"T+1 Settlement: are firms ready for 2027?","cachedAt":"2026-09-21T07:32:29.452Z","language":"en-GB","og:image":"https://www.fca.org.uk/sites/default/files/media/images/fca_social_share.jpg","og:title":"T+1 Settlement: are firms ready for 2027?","scrapeId":"01a0c314-82d7-7580-b4c4-ad5b9aaea0fd","viewport":"width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0","Generator":"Drupal 10 (https://www.drupal.org)","proxyUsed":"basic","sourceURL":"https://www.fca.org.uk/news/blogs/t1-settlement-are-firms-ready-2027","cacheState":"hit","ogSiteName":"FCA","statusCode":200,"contentType":"text/html; charset=UTF-8","creditsUsed":1,"description":"Halfway through the countdown to faster settlement for securities trades, Jamie Bell reflects on how ready firms really are for the change.","theme-color":["#fff","#6c1d45"],"modifiedTime":"2026-08-13T10:00:57+01:00","og:site_name":"FCA","twitter:card":"summary","twitter:site":"@TheFCA","msvalidate.01":"57E38F6BD7138599EBD7F7F4FF496957","ogDescription":"Why T+1 matters and what we’ve been doing so farThe UK’s move to a T+1 securities settlement cycle on 11 October 2027 is a fundamental shift in how securities transactions are settled.To prepare, market participants will have to rapidly speed up their post-trade processes, including automating their operations as appropriate.We’ve found that some are more ready than others – and some have a lot to do to meet deadlines, and our expectations.Participants should view T+1 as an opportunity. It will make the UK market more efficient and reduce risk – ultimately freeing up funds for investment.Our work on the T+1 transition is helping support growth in the UK financial market. We’re testing how ready market participants are for the transition, and where implementation challenges and bottlenecks remain.This forms part of our approach for T+1 we set out last year, alongside an active communications strategy and market monitoring.We’ve been speaking to participants across the market, including:buy-side and sell-side firmsfinancial market infrastructuresthird-party service providerstrade associationsThis blog sets out our reflections from that engagement.","publishedTime":"2026-08-06T10:55:05+01:00","twitter:image":"https://www.fca.org.uk/sites/default/files/images/fca-logo-social-media.jpg","twitter:title":"T+1 Settlement: are firms ready for 2027?","funnelback:nid":"172856","og:description":"Why T+1 matters and what we’ve been doing so farThe UK’s move to a T+1 securities settlement cycle on 11 October 2027 is a fundamental shift in how securities transactions are settled.To prepare, market participants will have to rapidly speed up their post-trade processes, including automating their operations as appropriate.We’ve found that some are more ready than others – and some have a lot to do to meet deadlines, and our expectations.Participants should view T+1 as an opportunity. It will make the UK market more efficient and reduce risk – ultimately freeing up funds for investment.Our work on the T+1 transition is helping support growth in the UK financial market. We’re testing how ready market participants are for the transition, and where implementation challenges and bottlenecks remain.This forms part of our approach for T+1 we set out last year, alongside an active communications strategy and market monitoring.We’ve been speaking to participants across the market, including:buy-side and sell-side firmsfinancial market infrastructuresthird-party service providerstrade associationsThis blog sets out our reflections from that engagement.","MobileOptimized":"width","funnelback:type":"Blogs,Markets","og:updated_time":"2026-08-13T10:00:57+01:00","twitter:site:id":"169431716","HandheldFriendly":"true","concurrencyLimited":false,"funnelback:category":"Markets","twitter:description":"Why T+1 matters and what we’ve been doing so farThe UK’s move to a T+1 securities settlement cycle on 11 October 2027 is a fundamental shift in how securities transactions are settled.To prepare, market participants will have to rapidly speed up their post-trade processes, including automating their operations as appropriate.We’ve found that some are more ready than others – and some have a lot to do to meet deadlines, and our expectations.Participants should view T+1 as an opportunity. It will make the UK market more efficient and reduce risk – ultimately freeing up funds for investment.Our work on the T+1 transition is helping support growth in the UK financial market. We’re testing how ready market participants are for the transition, and where implementation challenges and bottlenecks remain.This forms part of our approach for T+1 we set out last year, alongside an active communications strategy and market monitoring.We’ve been speaking to participants across the market, including:buy-side and sell-side firmsfinancial market infrastructuresthird-party service providerstrade associationsThis blog sets out our reflections from that engagement.","article:modified_time":"2026-08-13T10:00:57+01:00","funnelback:search-tag":"Growth","article:published_time":"2026-08-06T10:55:05+01:00","funnelback:search-type":"main,foi,news","mobile-web-app-capable":"yes","funnelback:content-type":"Blog","funnelback:created-date":"06/08/2026","funnelback:modified-date":"13/08/2026","google-site-verification":"UMTpd8qXqUbCGtkijm3SkFV2lRJQM2lIIkMKmJg8jMk","funnelback:published-date":"2026/08/13","funnelback:published-year":"2026","funnelback:published-int-date":"20260813","funnelback:date-of-release-year":"2026","apple-mobile-web-app-status-bar-style":"black"}},"success":true},"pairedItem":{"item":4}},{"json":{"data":{"markdown":"We use cookies to improve your online experience. Some cookies are necessary to make the site work. We’d like to set additional cookies to understand how the website is used. These are optional. All cookie data is anonymised. View [cookies](https://www.fca.org.uk/privacy/our-use-cookies \"Our use of cookies\").\n\n- Functional\n\nThese cookies are necessary for the operation of the FCA.org.uk web site\n\n- Non-functional\n\nAccept analytics and campaign advertising cookies\n\n\nAccept additional cookiesReject additional cookies\n\n# Whistleblowing quarterly data 2026 Q2\n\nDataFirst published: 27/08/2026Last updated: 27/08/2026\n\nOur data shows the number of new whistleblowing reports we received between April and June 2026, and existing reports closed during this period.\n\nThe data shows:\n\n- how we received the report\n- what the report was about\n- what level of action we have taken\n\nWe assess every whistleblowing report we receive that falls within our remit, to inform our work and help us identify actual or potential harm. This could be harm to consumers, to markets, to the UK economy or to wider society.\n\n## What we can share\n\nWe know that greater transparency about the whistleblowing reports we receive is important and we are constantly trying to improve the information we make public.\n\nHowever, our reviews will usually involve confidential information restricted by the Financial Services and Markets Act 2000 (FSMA), including that relating to our supervision and enforcement work. This restriction may limit the information we can share with whistleblowers.\n\n[Find out what we can share](https://www.fca.org.uk/freedom-information/information-we-can-share \"information we can share\")\n\n## Whistleblowing reports\n\nOur whistleblowing team receives reports by telephone, email, our online reporting form and post.\n\nIn this quarter (Q2 2026, April to June), we received 333 new whistleblowing reports.\n\nFor the same period in 2025 the team received 315 reports.\n\nIn Q1 2026 (January to March), we received 355 reports.\n\n**Chart tips**: hover over the data series to view the data values and filter the data categories by clicking on the legend\n\nChart\n\nData table\n\nDownload\n\nUse regions/landmarks to skip ahead to chart\n\n### Figure 1: Method of contact (2026 Q2). Source: FCA whistleblowing data\n\n#### Long description.\n\nNo description available.\n\n#### Structure.\n\nBar chart with 5 bars.\n\nThe chart has 1 X axis displaying categories.\n\nThe chart has 1 Y axis displaying Number of new reports.\n\n#### [View as data table.](https://www.fca.org.uk/data/whistleblowing-quarterly-data-2026-q2\\#highcharts-data-table-0)\n\n#### Chart graphic.\n\nFigure 1: Method of contact (2026 Q2)Created with Highcharts 6.1.0Number of new reportsFigure 1: Method of contact (2026 Q2)Source: FCA whistleblowing data17717799994444111122Online reporting formEmailTelephoneOtherLetter050100150200© Financial Conduct Authority 2026\n\n| Method of contact | Q4 2025 |\n| --- | --- |\n| Online reporting form | 177 |\n| Email | 99 |\n| Telephone | 44 |\n| Other | 11 |\n| Letter | 2 |\n\nFigure 1: Method of contact (2026 Q2)\n\nFigure 1 shows that we received the biggest proportion of our new reports in 2026 Q2 via our [online reporting form](https://www.fca.org.uk/firms/whistleblowing/how-make-report \"Whistleblowing: how to make a report\").\n\n## Contact details for whistleblowers\n\nProtecting the identities of the whistleblowers who contact us is vital. We understand individuals may be hesitant to share their personal information with us when making a disclosure.\n\nIt is helpful when whistleblowers provide us with an ongoing contact option such as a phone number and/or email address. This allows us to:\n\n- re-engage and develop on disclosures\n- ask further questions\n- keep individuals informed on how we can protect their identity whilst we carry out our work\n\nOur whistleblowing team reviews all reports to make sure we manage information from whistleblowers appropriately. We will redirect any information we get from consumers or firms to other relevant teams to consider, such as our Supervision Hub.\n\nChart\n\nData table\n\nDownload\n\nUse regions/landmarks to skip ahead to chart\n\n### Figure 2: Whistleblower confidentiality (2026 Q2). Source: FCA whistleblowing data\n\n#### Long description.\n\nNo description available.\n\n#### Structure.\n\nPie chart with 2 slices.\n\n#### [View as data table.](https://www.fca.org.uk/data/whistleblowing-quarterly-data-2026-q2\\#highcharts-data-table-1)\n\n#### Chart graphic.\n\nFigure 2: Whistleblower confidentiality (2026 Q2)Created with Highcharts 6.1.0Figure 2: Whistleblower confidentiality (2026 Q2)Source: FCA whistleblowing data31% (102)31% (102)69% (231)69% (231)AnonymousIdentity shared© Financial Conduct Authority 2026\n\n| Category | Whistleblowers |\n| --- | --- |\n| Anonymous | 31% (102) |\n| Identity shared | 69% (231) |\n\nFigure 2: Whistleblower confidentiality (2026 Q2)\n\nFigure 2 shows that in most of the reports we received in 2026 Q2, whistleblowers provided us with their contact details.\n\n## Whistleblowing allegations\n\nEvery report we receive will contain one or more allegations of wrongdoing. The 333 reports we received this quarter contained 886 allegations in total, with the following top 10:\n\nChart\n\nData table\n\nDownload\n\nUse regions/landmarks to skip ahead to chart\n\n### Figure 3: Top allegations reported by whistleblowers in Q2 2026. Source: FCA whistleblowing data\n\n#### Long description.\n\nNo description available.\n\n#### Structure.\n\nBar chart with 10 bars.\n\nThe chart has 1 X axis displaying Nature of allegation.\n\nThe chart has 1 Y axis displaying Reports.\n\n#### [View as data table.](https://www.fca.org.uk/data/whistleblowing-quarterly-data-2026-q2\\#highcharts-data-table-2)\n\n#### Chart graphic.\n\nFigure 3: Top allegations reported by whistleblowers in Q2 2026Created with Highcharts 6.1.0Nature of allegationReportsFigure 3: Top allegations reported by whistleblowers in Q2 2026Source: FCA whistleblowing data1971971531531211217373656540403131282827272121Consumer DutyLeadership & senior managers: behaviour, conduct & integritySystems and controlsIndividual(s): conduct, honesty, integrity & reputationFirm(s) – values & integrityUnauthorised business (permissions)FraudLoss or misuse of personal dataNon-financial misconductWhistleblowing systems & control0102030405060708090100110120130140150160170180190200210© Financial Conduct Authority 2026\n\n| Category | Reports |\n| --- | --- |\n| Consumer Duty | 197 |\n| Leadership & senior managers: behaviour, conduct & integrity | 153 |\n| Systems and controls | 121 |\n| Individual(s): conduct, honesty, integrity & reputation | 73 |\n| Firm(s) – values & integrity | 65 |\n| Unauthorised business (permissions) | 40 |\n| Fraud | 31 |\n| Loss or misuse of personal data | 28 |\n| Non-financial misconduct | 27 |\n| Whistleblowing systems & control | 21 |\n\nFigure 3: Top allegations reported by whistleblowers in Q2 2026\n\nIn Figure 3 we list the top 10 allegations made in whistleblowing reports between April and June 2026.\n\n## Closed whistleblowing reports\n\nWe closed 395 whistleblowing reports between April and June 2026.\n\n- Significant action to manage harm in 56 reports (14%) – this may include enforcement action, a section 166 [skilled person report](https://www.fca.org.uk/about/how-we-regulate/supervision/skilled-persons-reviews \"Skilled person reviews\"), or restricting a firm’s permissions or an individual’s approval.\n- Action to reduce harm in 114 reports (29%) – this may include writing to or visiting a firm, asking a firm for information, or asking a firm to attest to complying with our rules.\n- 193 reports (49%) informing our work, including harm prevention, but no direct action.\n- 14 reports (3%) not considered indicative of harm, but the information was recorded and will be available for future reference.\n- 18 reports classified as other (5%).\n\nWas this page useful?\n\nYes\n\nNo\n\nWhat can we do to improve pages like this?\n\nWhat did you find helpful?\n\nSubmit Feedback\n\nLeave this field blank\n\n[Print Page](https://www.fca.org.uk/data/whistleblowing-quarterly-data-2026-q2/printable/print \"Print Page\")\n\n[Linkedin](https://www.linkedin.com/sharing/share-offsite?mini=true&url=https%3A//www.fca.org.uk/data/whistleblowing-quarterly-data-2026-q2&title=Whistleblowing%20quarterly%20data%202026%20Q2&summary=&source=FCA \"Publish this post to LinkedIn\")\n\n[Twitter](https://twitter.com/share?url=https%3A//www.fca.org.uk/data/whistleblowing-quarterly-data-2026-q2&text=Whistleblowing%20quarterly%20data%202026%20Q2&summary=&source=FCA \"Share this on Twitter\")\n\n[Share with Email](mailto:?Subject=Whistleblowing%20quarterly%20data%202026%20Q2%20at%20FCA&Body=I%20saw%20this%20on%20the%20FCA%20website%20and%20thought%20you%20might%20be%20interested%20https%3A//www.fca.org.uk/data/whistleblowing-quarterly-data-2026-q2 \"Share with Email\")\n\nShare page\n\n\n## [Whistleblowing](https://www.fca.org.uk/data/whistleblowing-quarterly-data-2026-q2\\#relatedContentCollapse)\n\n[Homepage](https://www.fca.org.uk/firms/whistleblowing-make-a-report)\n\n[Legal advice and whistleblowing](https://www.fca.org.uk/firms/whistleblowing/legal-advice)\n\n[Previous whistleblowing data](https://www.fca.org.uk/firms/whistleblowing/data)\n\n## Data\n\n[View all FCA published data](https://www.fca.org.uk/data)\n\n- [Tweet](https://www.fca.org.uk/data/whistleblowing-quarterly-data-2026-q2 \"Share this selection on Twitter\")\n- [Facebook](https://www.fca.org.uk/data/whistleblowing-quarterly-data-2026-q2 \"Share this selection on Facebook\")\n- [LinkedIn](https://www.fca.org.uk/data/whistleblowing-quarterly-data-2026-q2 \"Share this selection on LinkedIn\")\n- [Share this selection by email](https://www.fca.org.uk/data/whistleblowing-quarterly-data-2026-q2 \"Share