"hit","ogSiteName":"FStech","statusCode":200,"contentType":"text/html; charset=UTF-8","creditsUsed":1,"description":"Visa has expanded its account-to-account (A2A) fraud prevention platform with new AI-powered capabilities designed to help financial institutions detect and stop fraudulent payments before money leaves customer accounts., Visa has expanded its account-to-account (A2A) fraud prevention platform with new AI-powered capabilities designed to help financial institutions detect and stop fraudulent payments before money leaves customer accounts.","og:site_name":"FStech","twitter:card":"summary_large_image","twitter:site":"@FStechnology","ogDescription":"Visa has expanded its account-to-account (A2A) fraud prevention platform with new AI-powered capabilities designed to help financial institutions detect and stop fraudulent payments before money leaves customer accounts.","twitter:title":"Visa updates A2A Protect to prevent fraud before payments leave accounts","article:author":"Dalvinder Kular","og:description":"Visa has expanded its account-to-account (A2A) fraud prevention platform with new AI-powered capabilities designed to help financial institutions detect and stop fraudulent payments before money leaves customer accounts.","twitter:domain":"https://www.fstech.co.uk","twitter:creator":"@FStechnology","twitter:image:src":"https://www.fstech.co.uk/fst/images/2026 Visa Logo.jpg","concurrencyLimited":false,"twitter:description":"Visa has expanded its account-to-account (A2A) fraud prevention platform with new AI-powered capabilities designed to help financial institutions detect and stop fraudulent payments before money leaves customer accounts."}},"success":true},"pairedItem":{"item":11}},{"json":{"data":{"markdown":"[![](https://www.fstech.co.uk/fst/images/728x90-Omnissa-banner-17-09-2026.jpg)](https://www.fstech.co.uk/fst/link.php?url=%20https://www.fstech.co.uk/autonomous-workplace-balancing-security-experience-and-control-in-financial-services/)\n\n#### Latest News\n\n- #### [Barclays staff push back against tighter return-to-office rules](https://www.fstech.co.uk/fst/Barclays_Staff_Push_Back_Against_Tighter_Return_to_Office_Rules.php)\n\n- #### [France ‘would back Dutch ECB president provided French chief economist elected’](https://www.fstech.co.uk/fst/France_Would_Back_Dutch_ECB_President_Provided_French_Chief_Economist_Elected.php)\n\n- #### [Revolut CEO says he wants dual UK-US listing for bank](https://www.fstech.co.uk/rs/Revolut_CEO_says_he_wants_dual_UK_US_listing_for_bank.php)\n\n- #### [Citi launches subscription management tool with Mastercard in UAE](https://www.fstech.co.uk/fst/Citi_Launches_Subscription_Management_Tool_With_Mastercard_In_UAE.php)\n\n- #### [Clarity Act fails to pass US Senate as Republicans turn on crypto bill](https://www.fstech.co.uk/fst/Clarity_Act_Fails_To_Pass_US_Senate_As_Republicans_Turn_On_Crypto_Bill.php)\n\n- #### [Revolut applies for Swiss banking licence, announces investment commitment](https://www.fstech.co.uk/fst/Revolut_applies_for_Swiss_banking_licence_announces_investment_commitment.php)\n\n\n[![](https://www.fstech.co.uk/fst/images/728x90-global-ai-awards-ofe-0926.gif)](https://www.fstech.co.uk/fst/link.php?url=%20https://www.nationaltechnology.co.uk/the-global-ai-awards/enter_the_awards.php)\n\n## Lenders accuse FCA of working backwards on £9.1bn redress scheme\n\nBy Jonathan Easton01/09/2026\n\nMotor finance lenders have accused the Financial Conduct Authority (FCA) of “working backwards” to justify its £9.1 billion redress scheme, as legal challenges to the compensation programme continue ahead of hearings later this year.\n\nMercedes-Benz Financial Services, Volkswagen Financial Services and Crédit Agricole Auto Finance are challenging the scheme at the Upper Tribunal, arguing that the regulator has misinterpreted the Supreme Court judgment that opened the way for industry-wide compensation.\n\nMercedes-Benz Financial Services, which has set aside up to £400 million for potential payouts, said the FCA’s “one-size-fits-all approach” across the motor finance market “risks sowing the seeds of failure for the scheme as a whole”. It accused the regulator of reaching an initial conclusion that commission arrangements were harmful before developing its approach to support that position.\n\nThe legal challenge follows a Supreme Court ruling last year which rejected the argument that undisclosed commissions were automatically unlawful, while finding that one borrower’s undisclosed commission had created an “unfair relationship”. The judgment allowed the FCA to develop a broader redress scheme covering motor finance agreements issued between 2007 and 2024.\n\nThe FCA has defended its approach, with chief executive Nikhil Rathi saying in March: “It’s time for lenders to put right the fact they broke the law.” The regulator has said it will defend the scheme robustly, while parts of the programme were suspended in July pending the legal challenges.\n\nThe suspension means lenders do not currently have to calculate or pay compensation, or contact eligible consumers about redress, according to the FCA. If the scheme survives the legal process, payments could begin in 2027, while a revised scheme could push compensation into 2028 or later.\n\nConsumer Voice, which is separately challenging the scheme on behalf of consumers, has argued that the proposed average payout of £830 per affected loan is insufficient. The group’s co-founder Alex Neill said: “We remain resolute and confident in our challenge on behalf of millions of consumers who are being short-changed by the redress scheme.”\n\nThe FCA has disputed Consumer Voice’s position and is seeking to challenge the group’s application, citing concerns about its funding and commercial relationships. The regulator has said it continued engaging with the organisation after an April meeting that Consumer Voice alleges involved warnings about the consequences of pursuing legal action.\n\nHearings in the wider legal challenge are expected in December or February 2027, with the outcome determining whether the FCA’s proposed £9.1bn framework survives or is replaced.\n\n* * *\n\nShare Story:\n\n### Recent Stories\n\n### [Bank of England given new payments innovation objective](https://www.fstech.co.uk/fst/Bank_Of_England_Given_New_Payments_Innovation_Objective.php?utm_source=jsrecent)\n\n[READ STORY](https://www.fstech.co.uk/fst/Bank_Of_England_Given_New_Payments_Innovation_Objective.php?utm_source=jsrecent)\n\n### [Lenders accuse FCA of working backwards on £9.1bn redress scheme](https://www.fstech.co.uk/fst/Lenders_Accuse_FCA_Of_Working_Backwards_On_9_1bn_Redress_Scheme.php?utm_source=jsrecent)\n\n[READ STORY](https://www.fstech.co.uk/fst/Lenders_Accuse_FCA_Of_Working_Backwards_On_9_1bn_Redress_Scheme.php?utm_source=jsrecent)\n\n### [US bank regulators narrow enforcement focus to financial risks](https://www.fstech.co.uk/fst/US_Bank_Regulators_Narrow_Enforcement_Focus_To_Financial_Risks.php?utm_source=jsrecent)\n\n[READ STORY](https://www.fstech.co.uk/fst/US_Bank_Regulators_Narrow_Enforcement_Focus_To_Financial_Risks.php?utm_source=jsrecent)\n\n[![](https://www.fstech.co.uk/fst/images/Static-banner-728x90-01-09-2026.png)](https://www.fstech.co.uk/fst/link.php?url=%20https://www.fstech.co.uk/fst/the-safeguarding-playbook/)\n\nCreating value together: Strategic partnerships in the age of GCCs\n\nYour browser does not support HTML5 video.\n\nAs Global Capability Centres reshape the financial services landscape, one question stands out: how do leading banks balance in-house innovation with strategic partnerships to drive real transformation?\n\n[![BANNER](https://www.fstech.co.uk/fst/images/300x250_FStech_cyber_conf_2025-Overview-1225.jpg)](https://www.fstech.co.uk/Online/FStechCyberSecurityConference-Overview2025.pdf)\n\n[![BANNER](https://www.fstech.co.uk/fst/images/TFOAI-overview-1225.gif)](https://www.perspectivepublishing.co.uk/dev/cms/uploads/FStech%20-%20Future%20AI%20Finserv/2026/fstechfutureofaiconference2025-overview.pdf)\n\nData trust in the AI era: Building customer confidence through responsible banking\n\nYour browser does not support HTML5 video.\n\nIn the second episode of FStech’s three-part video podcast series sponsored by HCLTech, Sudip Lahiri, Executive Vice President & Head of Financial Services for Europe & UKI at HCLTech examines the critical relationship between data trust, transparency, and responsible AI implementation in financial services.\n\nBanking's GenAI evolution: Beyond the hype, building the future\n\nYour browser does not support HTML5 video.\n\nIn the first episode of a three-part video podcast series sponsored by HCLTech, Sudip Lahiri, Executive Vice President & Head of Financial Services for Europe & UKI at HCLTech explores how financial institutions can navigate the transformative potential of Generative AI while building lasting foundations for innovation.\n\n- [Popular](https://www.fstech.co.uk/fst/Lenders_Accuse_FCA_Of_Working_Backwards_On_9_1bn_Redress_Scheme.php#solid-justified-tab1)\n- [Recent](https://www.fstech.co.uk/fst/Lenders_Accuse_FCA_Of_Working_Backwards_On_9_1bn_Redress_Scheme.php#solid-justified-tab2)\n\n- [1\\\\\n\\\\\nRevolut confirms customer data breach after fake government requests](https://www.fstech.co.uk/fst/Revolut_Confirms_Customer_Data_Breach_After_Fake_Government_Requests.php)\n- [2\\\\\n\\\\\nOpenAI launches ChatGPT for financial services](https://www.fstech.co.uk/fst/OpenAI_launches_ChatGPT_for_financial_services.php)\n- [3\\\\\n\\\\\nHSBC CFO Pam Kaur to step down](https://www.fstech.co.uk/fst/HSBC_CFO_Pam_Kaur_To_Step_Down.php)\n- [4\\\\\n\\\\\nMetro Bank launches new payments partnership to help businesses with cashflow](https://www.fstech.co.uk/fst/Metro_Bank_Launches_New_Payments_Partnership_To_Help_Businesses_With_Cashflow.php)\n- [5\\\\\n\\\\\nCrispin Odey loses appeal against City ban and £1.5m fine](https://www.fstech.co.uk/fst/Crispin_Odey_Loses_Appeal_Against_City_Ban.php)\n- [6\\\\\n\\\\\nWells Fargo commercial banking EVP set to retire](https://www.fstech.co.uk/fst/Wells_Fargo_Commercial_Banking_EVP_Set_To_Retire.php)\n- [7\\\\\n\\\\\nSenate Republicans introduce ethics text to Clarity Act on eve of vote](https://www.fstech.co.uk/fst/Senate_Republicans_Introduce_Ethics_Text_To_Clarity_Act_On_Eve_Of_Vote.php)\n- [8\\\\\n\\\\\nKlarna launches in-app call verification feature to fight fraud](https://www.fstech.co.uk/fst/Klarna_Launches_Inapp_Call_Verification_Feature_To_Fight_Fraud.php)\n- [9\\\\\n\\\\\nBarclays facing up to £37m bill over accounts provided to ‘Ponzi scheme’](https://www.fstech.co.uk/fst/Barclays_Facing_Up_To_37m_Bill_Over_Accounts_Provided_To_Ponzi_Scheme.php)\n- [10\\\\\n\\\\\nBarclays staff push back against tighter return-to-office rules](https://www.fstech.co.uk/fst/Barclays_Staff_Push_Back_Against_Tighter_Return_to_Office_Rules.php)\n\n- [1\\\\\n\\\\\nFrance ‘would back Dutch ECB president provided French chief economist elected’](https://www.fstech.co.uk/fst/France_Would_Back_Dutch_ECB_President_Provided_French_Chief_Economist_Elected.php)\n- [2\\\\\n\\\\\nCiti launches subscription management tool with Mastercard in UAE](https://www.fstech.co.uk/fst/Citi_Launches_Subscription_Management_Tool_With_Mastercard_In_UAE.php)\n- [3\\\\\n\\\\\nRevolut CEO says he wants dual UK-US listing for bank](https://www.fstech.co.uk/rs/Revolut_CEO_says_he_wants_dual_UK_US_listing_for_bank.php)\n- [4\\\\\n\\\\\nBarclays staff push back against tighter return-to-office rules](https://www.fstech.co.uk/fst/Barclays_Staff_Push_Back_Against_Tighter_Return_to_Office_Rules.php)\n- [5\\\\\n\\\\\nZopa launches AI banking assistant which uses natural language](https://www.fstech.co.uk/fst/Zopa_Launches_AI_Banking_Assistant_Which_Uses_Natural_Language.php)\n- [6\\\\\n\\\\\nRevolut applies for Swiss banking licence, announces investment commitment](https://www.fstech.co.uk/fst/Revolut_applies_for_Swiss_banking_licence_announces_investment_commitment.php)\n- [7\\\\\n\\\\\nClarity Act fails to pass US Senate as Republicans turn on crypto bill](https://www.fstech.co.uk/fst/Clarity_Act_Fails_To_Pass_US_Senate_As_Republicans_Turn_On_Crypto_Bill.php)\n- [8\\\\\n\\\\\nWorld Bank names Nobel laureate Michael Kremer as chief economist](https://www.fstech.co.uk/fst/World_Bank_Names_Nobel_Laureate_Michael_Kremer_As.php)\n- [9\\\\\n\\\\\nFCA seeking industry input on tokenising gold](https://www.fstech.co.uk/fst/FCA_seeking_industry_input_on_tokenising_gold.php)\n- [10\\\\\n\\\\\nKlarna launches in-app call verification feature to fight fraud](https://www.fstech.co.uk/fst/Klarna_Launches_Inapp_Call_Verification_Feature_To_Fight_Fraud.php)\n\n- [Expert Views](https://www.fstech.co.uk/fst/Lenders_Accuse_FCA_Of_Working_Backwards_On_9_1bn_Redress_Scheme.php#solid-justified-tabexpertviews)\n\n- [1\\\\\n\\\\\nFinance AI is about honesty, not personality](https://www.fstech.co.uk/fst/finance-ai-is-about-honesty-not-personality.php)\n- [2\\\\\n\\\\\nComplaint handling is the right place to put AI to work](https://www.fstech.co.uk/fst/expert-views-complaint-handling-is-the-right-place-to-put-ai-to-work.php)\n- [3\\\\\n\\\\\nShorten the path from financial statements to credit decisions – How AI is Closing the gap in commercial lending](https://www.fstech.co.uk/fst/shorten-the-path-from-financial-statements-to-credit-decisions.php)\n- [4\\\\\n\\\\\nBuild, buy or partner? Why the right AI strategy is the one built for your business](https://www.fstech.co.uk/fst/build-buy-or-partner-why-the-right-ai-strategy-is-the-one-built-for-your-business.php)\n- [5\\\\\n\\\\\nOpenAI Didn’t Fail at Checkout… Payments Did](https://www.fstech.co.uk/fst/expert-view-openai-didnt-fail-at-checkout-payments-did.php)\n- [6\\\\\n\\\\\nBuilding a Security First Culture in Retail Banking](https://www.fstech.co.uk/fst/expert-views-building-a-security-first-culture-in-retail-banking.php)\n- [7\\\\\n\\\\\nHow the CIO and CFO partnership has evolved in the digital age](https://www.fstech.co.uk/fst/how-the-financial-services-cio-and-cfo-partnership-has-evolved-in-the-digital-age.php)\n- [8\\\\\n\\\\\nBuilding the future of intelligent finance: Huawei’s vision for a digital financial ecosystem](https://www.fstech.co.uk/fst/huawei-building-the-future-of-intelligent-finance-expert-view.php)\n\nBeyond compliance: Building unshakeable operational resilience in financial services\n\nYour browser does not support HTML5 video.\n\nIn today's rapidly evolving financial landscape, operational resilience has become a critical focus for institutions worldwide. As regulatory requirements grow more complex and cyber threats, particularly ransomware, become increasingly sophisticated, financial services providers must adapt and strengthen their defences. The intersection of compliance, technology, and security presents both challenges and opportunities.\n\n[![](https://www.fstech.co.uk/fst/images/Finance-Ad-120x600-20260731.png)](https://www.fstech.co.uk/fst/link.php?url=%20https://www.fstech.co.uk/fst/risk-resilience-and-the-modern-financial-workspace/)\n\n[![](https://www.fstech.co.uk/fst/images/120x600-FStechEvent-banner-17-08-2026.jpg)](https://www.fstech.co.uk/fst/link.php?url=%20https://www.fstech.co.uk/fsi-leadership-forum-navigating-ai-sovereignty-and-the-next-era-of-banking/)\n\n[![](https://www.fstech.co.uk/fst/images/120x600-A-rackspace-keep-control-ai-0526.png)](https://www.fstech.co.uk/fst/link.php?url=%20https://www.fstech.co.uk/fst/scaling-sovereign-ai-across-uk-financial-services/)\n\n[![](https://www.fstech.co.uk/fst/images/payments-awards-book-now-120X600-Book-0726.jpg)](https://www.fstech.co.uk/fst/link.php?url=%20https://www.payments-awards.com/awards/booking_form.php)\n\n[![](https://www.fstech.co.uk/fst/images/120x600-FStechAI-Register-animated-0625.gif)](https://www.fstech.co.uk/fst/link.php?url=%20https://www.fstech.co.uk/futureai-finserv/)\n\n[![](https://www.fstech.co.uk/fst/images/120x600_FStech_cyber_conf_2026-register-now-0625.gif)](https://www.fstech.co.uk/fst/link.php?url=%20https://www.fstech.co.uk/cybersecuritylive/)\n\n[![](https://www.fstech.co.uk/fst/images/120x600-FStechawards2027-Open-animated-0826.gif)](https://www.fstech.co.uk/fst/link.php?url=%20https://www.fstech.co.uk/awards/enter_the_awards.php)\n\nWe use cookies to ensure you get the best experience on our website. [Learn More](http://www.perspectivepublishing.com/privacy_policy.html)\nAccept\n\n\n© 2019 Perspective Publishing     [Privacy & Cookies](http://www.perspectivepublishing.com/privacy_policy.html)\n\n![](<Base64-Image-Removed>)\n\n[To Top](https://www.fstech.co.uk/fst/Lenders_Accuse_FCA_Of_Working_Backwards_On_9_1bn_Redress_Scheme.php#)","metadata":{"url":"https://www.fstech.co.uk/fst/Lenders_Accuse_FCA_Of_Working_Backwards_On_9_1bn_Redress_Scheme.php","name":"Lenders accuse FCA of working backwards on £9.1bn redress scheme","image":"https://www.fstech.co.uk/fst/images/FCA (1).jpg","ogUrl":"https://www.fstech.co.uk/fst/Lenders_Accuse_FCA_Of_Working_Backwards_On_9_1bn_Redress_Scheme.php","title":"Lenders accuse FCA of working backwards on £9.1bn redress scheme - FStech","author":"Jonathan Easton","og:url":"https://www.fstech.co.uk/fst/Lenders_Accuse_FCA_Of_Working_Backwards_On_9_1bn_Redress_Scheme.php","favicon":"https://www.fstech.co.uk/favicon.ico","og:type":"article","ogImage":"https://www.fstech.co.uk/fst/images/FCA (1).jpg","ogTitle":"Lenders accuse FCA of working backwards on £9.1bn redress scheme","cachedAt":"2026-09-21T08:25:23.318Z","keywords":"FCA, motor finance, redress scheme, consumer compensation, car finance","language":"en","og:image":"https://www.fstech.co.uk/fst/images/FCA (1).jpg","og:title":"Lenders accuse FCA of working backwards on £9.1bn redress scheme","ogLocale":"en_GB","scrapeId":"01a0c315-4ea5-71d8-93bc-d20da85ce063","viewport":"width=device-width, initial-scale=1","og:locale":"en_GB","proxyUsed":"basic","sourceURL":"http://www.fstech.co.uk/fst/Lenders_Accuse_FCA_Of_Working_Backwards_On_9_1bn_Redress_Scheme.php","cacheState":"hit","ogSiteName":"FStech","statusCode":200,"contentType":"text/html; charset=UTF-8","creditsUsed":1,"description":"Motor finance lenders have accused the Financial Conduct Authority (FCA) of “working backwards” to justify its £9.1 billion redress scheme, Motor finance lenders have accused the Financial Conduct Authority (FCA) of “working backwards” to justify its £9.1 billion redress scheme","og:site_name":"FStech","twitter:card":"summary_large_image","twitter:site":"@FStechnology","ogDescription":"Motor finance lenders have accused the Financial Conduct Authority (FCA) of “working backwards” to justify its £9.1 billion redress scheme","twitter:title":"Lenders accuse FCA of working backwards on £9.1bn redress scheme","article:author":"Jonathan Easton","og:description":"Motor finance lenders have accused the Financial Conduct Authority (FCA) of “working backwards” to justify its £9.1 billion redress scheme","twitter:domain":"https://www.fstech.co.uk","twitter:creator":"@FStechnology","twitter:image:src":"https://www.fstech.co.uk/fst/images/FCA (1).jpg","concurrencyLimited":false,"twitter:description":"Motor finance lenders have accused the Financial Conduct Authority (FCA) of “working backwards” to justify its £9.1 billion redress scheme"}},"success":true},"pairedItem":{"item":12}},{"json":{"data":{"markdown":"­\n\nFirms still struggling to prove Consumer Duty outcomes, CII finds\n\n [Skip to content](https://www.moneymarketing.co.uk/news/firms-still-struggling-to-prove-consumer-duty-outcomes-cii-finds/#content)\n\nView more on these topics [Advisers](https://www.moneymarketing.co.uk/advisers/) [Regulation](https://www.moneymarketing.co.uk/regulation/) [News](https://www.moneymarketing.co.uk/news/)\n\nFinancial advice and insurance firms are still struggling to demonstrate that customers are receiving good outcomes under the Consumer Duty, despite becoming more confident in their board reporting, the Chartered Insurance Institute (CII) has found.\n\nAround three-quarters of firms highlighted difficulties with integrating and sourcing appropriate data and connecting different datasets, according to the CII’s latest roundtable on Consumer Duty board reporting.\n\nThe professional body said challenges stemming from firms’ unfamiliarity with the regulator’s expectations have eased since the introduction of the Duty, but more practical and structural problems remain.\n\nIn particular, firms are continuing to grapple with how to demonstrate that customers understand the products and services they are using.\n\nThe CII found processes for responding when a customer misunderstanding has already been identified were considerably more developed than mechanisms for detecting misunderstanding in the first place.\n\nParticipants said firms needed better testing at key customer decision points, clearer measures of outcomes and a more joined-up approach to using customer behaviour data.\n\nThe findings are contained in the CII report, Consumer Duty Board reporting: two years on – from reporting to proving outcomes, following its third annual roundtable on the subject.\n\nThe July session brought together representatives from the Financial Conduct Authority (FCA) alongside participants from insurance, financial planning, compliance, customer insight and professional standards.\n\nThe FCA reiterated that firms should move away from treating their Consumer Duty board reports primarily as a compliance exercise and instead use them as governance tools to identify problems and drive improvements in customer outcomes.\n\n[Vicky Pearce: Making Consumer Duty evidence count](https://www.moneymarketing.co.uk/opinion/vicky-pearce-making-consumer-duty-evidence-count/)\n\nParticipants also raised concerns about proportionality and the expectations placed on smaller firms.\n\nThey argued smaller businesses can have an advantage because they are often closer to their clients and better positioned to investigate individual outcomes.\n\nHowever, they may lack the scale, internal independence and research resources available to larger organisations when testing consumer understanding and gathering evidence of outcomes.\n\nCII executive director of strategy, advocacy and professional standards Adam Harper said: “The real test of Consumer Duty Board reporting is whether firms can use the evidence to understand where customers are experiencing poor outcomes and take meaningful action to improve them.\n\n“The CII will build on this work with the development of further practical guidance to support firms as we strengthen our sector’s delivery in accordance with Consumer Duty.”\n\nThe CII said it will continue working with members, firms and the regulator to develop sector-specific support reflecting the practical challenges businesses face.\n\nThe work will focus on helping firms use Consumer Duty reporting as a tool for continuous improvement rather than simply demonstrating regulatory compliance.\n\n### Comments\n\nThere are 2 comments at the moment, we would love to hear your opinion too.\n\n1. ![b05fdbfbbb14a448f02bfa279b6dd771f49623c3b24860621f3feb9da4ece473?s=56&d=mm&r=g](https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/b05fdbfbbb14a448f02bfa279b6dd771f49623c3b24860621f3feb9da4ece473?s=56&d=mm&r=g)\n\n\n\n\n\nHarry Katz [2nd September 2026 at 1:16 pm](https://www.moneymarketing.co.uk/news/firms-still-struggling-to-prove-consumer-duty-outcomes-cii-finds/#comment-167288)\n\n\n\nThis seems to highlight the problems the big firms are having. It would seem that yet again it is the smaller firms that can give their clients the best outcomes. Not difficult to give clients a questionnaire in addition to other thing, particularly the closeness with the client and continuity of adviser. Something the big boys would find difficult. They want to pile it high and sell it expensive. After all they have legions on the payroll. Non productive managers (those that don’t interact directly with clients), paraplanners, receptionists, all manner of spurious jobs and titles. (Client liaison, acquisition managers and so forth) all having to be paid for. I would guess that consumer duty was formulated precisely for the bigger firms. Odd that the regulator eschews the little guy in preference to the big boys. But then perhaps not so surprising – it is the big firms that can offer them a cushy number when they leave the regulator, as been demonstrated numerous times already.\n\n\n\n[Log in to Reply](https://www.moneymarketing.co.uk/wordpress/wp-login.php?redirect_to=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.moneymarketing.co.uk%2Fnews%2Ffirms-still-struggling-to-prove-consumer-duty-outcomes-cii-finds%2F)\n\n2. ![ec766f915e11c673eacddacc4c00f0fcab9a84e3fb40a8fb9ddbe07bd59ea5ca?s=56&d=mm&r=g](https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/ec766f915e11c673eacddacc4c00f0fcab9a84e3fb40a8fb9ddbe07bd59ea5ca?s=56&d=mm&r=g)\n\n\n\n\n\nJulian Stevens [2nd September 2026 at 4:44 pm](https://www.moneymarketing.co.uk/news/firms-still-struggling-to-prove-consumer-duty-outcomes-cii-finds/#comment-167291)\n\n\n\nEasier said than done. But the FCA goons don’t care about that. It’s just more procedural crap with which to burden the adviser community.\n\n\n\n[Log in to Reply](https://www.moneymarketing.co.uk/wordpress/wp-login.php?redirect_to=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.moneymarketing.co.uk%2Fnews%2Ffirms-still-struggling-to-prove-consumer-duty-outcomes-cii-finds%2F)\n\n\n### Leave a comment\n\n[Cancel reply](https://www.moneymarketing.co.uk/news/firms-still-struggling-to-prove-consumer-duty-outcomes-cii-finds/#respond)\n\nYou must be [logged in](https://www.moneymarketing.co.uk/news/firms-still-struggling-to-prove-consumer-duty-outcomes-cii-finds/#header-login) to post a comment.\n\n![cross.png](https://www.moneymarketing.co.uk/content/plugins/yeloni-free-exit-popup/common/images/cross.png)\n\n##### Subscribe to our newsletters\n\nSign up to Money Marketing news alerts and keep up-