il\")\n\n- [Tweet](https://www.fca.org.uk/news/speeches/building-stronger-uk-investment-culture \"Share this selection on Twitter\")\n- [Facebook](https://www.fca.org.uk/news/speeches/building-stronger-uk-investment-culture \"Share this selection on Facebook\")\n- [LinkedIn](https://www.fca.org.uk/news/speeches/building-stronger-uk-investment-culture \"Share this selection on LinkedIn\")\n- [Share this selection by email](https://www.fca.org.uk/news/speeches/building-stronger-uk-investment-culture \"Share this selection by email\")\n\nShare this selection\n\n- [Tweet](https://www.fca.org.uk/news/speeches/building-stronger-uk-investment-culture \"Share this selection on Twitter\")\n- [Facebook](https://www.fca.org.uk/news/speeches/building-stronger-uk-investment-culture \"Share this selection on Facebook\")\n- [LinkedIn](https://www.fca.org.uk/news/speeches/building-stronger-uk-investment-culture \"Share this selection on LinkedIn\")\n- [Share this selection by email](https://www.fca.org.uk/news/speeches/building-stronger-uk-investment-culture \"Share this selection by email\")","metadata":{"url":"https://www.fca.org.uk/news/speeches/building-stronger-uk-investment-culture","ogUrl":"https://www.fca.org.uk/news/speeches/building-stronger-uk-investment-culture","title":"Building a stronger UK investment culture | FCA","og:url":"https://www.fca.org.uk/news/speeches/building-stronger-uk-investment-culture","favicon":"https://www.fca.org.uk/themes/custom/fca/favicon.ico","og:type":"article","ogImage":"https://www.fca.org.uk/sites/default/files/media/images/fca_social_share.jpg","ogTitle":"Building a stronger UK investment culture","cachedAt":"2026-09-21T01:32:24.566Z","language":"en-GB","og:image":"https://www.fca.org.uk/sites/default/files/media/images/fca_social_share.jpg","og:title":"Building a stronger UK investment culture","scrapeId":"01a0c315-9af0-72d9-9cac-5d3a41c9e471","viewport":"width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0","Generator":"Drupal 10 (https://www.drupal.org)","proxyUsed":"basic","sourceURL":"https://www.fca.org.uk/news/speeches/building-stronger-uk-investment-culture","cacheState":"hit","ogSiteName":"FCA","statusCode":200,"contentType":"text/html; charset=UTF-8","creditsUsed":1,"description":"Speech by Lucy Castledine, director of consumer investments, at the 2026 Investor Summit, London.","theme-color":["#fff","#6c1d45"],"modifiedTime":"2026-09-18T10:03:13+01:00","og:site_name":"FCA","twitter:card":"summary","twitter:site":"@TheFCA","msvalidate.01":"57E38F6BD7138599EBD7F7F4FF496957","ogDescription":"Speech by Lucy Castledine, director of consumer investments, at the 2026 Investor Summit. ","publishedTime":"2026-09-16T08:58:16+01:00","twitter:image":"https://www.fca.org.uk/sites/default/files/images/fca-logo-social-media.jpg","twitter:title":"Building a stronger UK investment culture","funnelback:nid":"174006","og:description":"Speech by Lucy Castledine, director of consumer investments, at the 2026 Investor Summit. ","MobileOptimized":"width","funnelback:type":"Speeches","og:updated_time":"2026-09-18T10:03:13+01:00","twitter:site:id":"169431716","HandheldFriendly":"true","concurrencyLimited":false,"twitter:description":"Speech by Lucy Castledine, director of consumer investments, at the 2026 Investor Summit. ","article:modified_time":"2026-09-18T10:03:13+01:00","funnelback:search-tag":"Growth|Financial crime","article:published_time":"2026-09-16T08:58:16+01:00","funnelback:search-type":"main,foi,news","mobile-web-app-capable":"yes","funnelback:content-type":"News","funnelback:created-date":"16/09/2026","funnelback:modified-date":"18/09/2026","google-site-verification":"UMTpd8qXqUbCGtkijm3SkFV2lRJQM2lIIkMKmJg8jMk","funnelback:published-date":"2026/09/18","funnelback:published-year":"2026","funnelback:published-int-date":"20260918","funnelback:date-of-release-year":"2026","funnelback:historical-publication":"false","apple-mobile-web-app-status-bar-style":"black"}},"success":true},"pairedItem":{"item":15}},{"json":{"data":{"markdown":"We use cookies to improve your online experience. Some cookies are necessary to make the site work. We’d like to set additional cookies to understand how the website is used. These are optional. All cookie data is anonymised. View [cookies](https://www.fca.org.uk/privacy/our-use-cookies \"Our use of cookies\").\n\n- Functional\n\nThese cookies are necessary for the operation of the FCA.org.uk web site\n\n- Non-functional\n\nAccept analytics and campaign advertising cookies\n\n\nAccept additional cookiesReject additional cookies\n\n17 September 2026\n\n![clock icon](https://www.fca.org.uk/themes/custom/fca/images/clock.svg) 3 minutes\nreading time\n\n- [Consumer Duty](https://www.fca.org.uk/search-results?topic_tags=f.Tag%7CfcaSearchTag%3DConsumer%20Duty&sort_by=dmetaZ)\n\n# Better outcomes begin when payment firms understand customers’ needs\n\n![Headshot of Alison Russell](https://www.fca.org.uk/sites/default/files/styles/thumbnail/public/media/images/alison-russell.jpg.webp?itok=-_gRHJCd)\n\nAlison Russell\n\nHead of cross-cutting delivery\n\nOur review of consumer vulnerability found that the firms getting the best outcomes were taking simple steps to understand customers, understand vulnerability, and design their support around them.\n\n![Two customer service advisers wearing headsets working at computers in an office.](https://www.fca.org.uk/sites/default/files/styles/blog_main_image_desktop/public/media/images/customer-support-contact-centre.jpg.jpg?itok=xr4gX3qD)\n\nDuring our review of consumer vulnerability, we saw how relatively simple changes can make a real difference. One payments provider serving small business customers found that some customers were struggling to set up and manage their accounts because of limited IT literacy.\n\nThe firm responded by arranging callback support to help customers set up their accounts.\n\nAs a result, customers were able to manage their accounts successfully, without needing to invest in expensive new systems or roll out elaborate new processes.\n\n## Investing in time, not in systems\n\nPayments are woven into everyday life. They're how people receive their salary, pay bills, support family members and manage their finances.\n\nWhen customers are in vulnerable circumstances, barriers to accessing or using payment services can have a significant impact on their daily lives. That's why it's important that firms understand their customers' needs and design support that helps them access and use services effectively.\n\nOur [consumer vulnerability review](https://www.fca.org.uk/publications/good-and-poor-practice/payments-firms-delivering-good-outcomes-vulnerable-consumers \"Payments firms: delivering good outcomes for consumers in vulnerable circumstances\") found that firms achieving the best outcomes had invested time in understanding their customers and adapting support to meet their needs.\n\nUnder the [Consumer Duty](https://www.fca.org.uk/firms/consumer-duty/about \"About the Consumer Duty\"), firms should act to deliver good outcomes for retail customers, including those in vulnerable circumstances.\n\nAround half of UK adults show at least one characteristic of vulnerability, so this isn't a niche issue for a handful of firms.\n\nIt's something every payments and e-money firm needs to think about.\n\n## Design, adapt, and test\n\nWe found that firms who understood their customers were better able to design support around real needs, adapt their communications, and ensure people received a consistent experience.\n\nThis helped them deliver better outcomes, reduce barriers to access and support customers to make effective use of products and services.\n\nAnd they were able to do this in simple, customer-centred ways.\n\nAlmost every firm we looked at could point to policies, training materials or procedures. These are a good start.\n\nBut these alone don’t guarantee customers will get good outcomes.\n\nThe most successful firms went a step further. They asked whether those arrangements were really working in practice, and checked.\n\nThey used management information, complaints data, customer feedback and quality assurance to understand how customers in vulnerable circumstances were actually experiencing their products and services.\n\nIf something wasn't working, they changed it.\n\n## Simple changes\n\nFor smaller firms, this doesn't need to mean sophisticated reporting infrastructure.\n\nIt can be as simple as your board or senior management regularly reviewing how many customers in vulnerable circumstances you're identifying, what kinds of vulnerability you're seeing, and how that links to your complaints data.\n\nA handful of firms did exactly this, and it helped them spot where they were doing well and where they needed to improve.\n\n## Five questions every firm should be able to answer\n\nFirms can be flexible in how they meet their obligations under the Duty. But reflecting on what we found, I think every firm, whatever its size, should be able to answer a few simple questions:\n\n1. Do we understand the vulnerabilities most relevant to our customers?\n2. Are we identifying and supporting customers consistently?\n3. Can we demonstrate that our approach is leading to good outcomes?\n4. Where something isn't working, are we learning and improving?\n5. Does our board or senior management receive enough information to understand whether customers in vulnerable circumstances are receiving good outcomes?\n\nThe answers will look different from firm to firm, but the goal is the same for everyone: helping consumers, including those in vulnerable circumstances, receive good outcomes when using payments services.\n\nNone of this requires lots of resources or expensive systems. It requires asking the 5 questions above, and acting on the answers.\n\nOur full findings are in our published review: [Payments firms: delivering good outcomes for consumers in vulnerable circumstances](https://www.fca.org.uk/publications/good-and-poor-practice/payments-firms-delivering-good-outcomes-vulnerable-consumers \"Payments firms: delivering good outcomes for consumers in vulnerable circumstances  \").\n\nIf you run a smaller payments or e-money firm, the sections on considerations for smaller firms are designed with you in mind: they set out proportionate approaches that still deliver good outcomes.\n\nI hope firms will reflect on these findings, continue the practices that are working well, and make changes where improvements are needed.\n\n[Print Page](https://www.fca.org.uk/news/blogs/better-outcomes-payment-firms-understand-customer-needs/printable/print \"Print Page\")\n\n[Linkedin](https://www.linkedin.com/sharing/share-offsite?mini=true&url=https%3A//www.fca.org.uk/news/blogs/better-outcomes-payment-firms-understand-customer-needs&title=Better%20outcomes%20begin%20when%20payment%20firms%20understand%20customers%E2%80%99%20needs&summary=&source=FCA \"Publish this post to LinkedIn\")\n\n[Twitter](https://twitter.com/share?url=https%3A//www.fca.org.uk/news/blogs/better-outcomes-payment-firms-understand-customer-needs&text=Better%20outcomes%20begin%20when%20payment%20firms%20understand%20customers%E2%80%99%20needs&summary=&source=FCA \"Share this on Twitter\")\n\n[Share with Email](mailto:?Subject=Better%20outcomes%20begin%20when%20payment%20firms%20understand%20customers%E2%80%99%20needs%20at%20FCA&Body=I%20saw%20this%20on%20the%20FCA%20website%20and%20thought%20you%20might%20be%20interested%20https%3A//www.fca.org.uk/news/blogs/better-outcomes-payment-firms-understand-customer-needs \"Share with Email\")\n\nShare page\n\n\nWas this page useful?\n\nYes\n\nNo\n\nWhat can we do to improve pages like this?\n\nWhat did you find helpful?\n\nSubmit Feedback\n\nLeave this field blank\n\n- [Tweet](https://www.fca.org.uk/news/blogs/better-outcomes-payment-firms-understand-customer-needs \"Share this selection on Twitter\")\n- [Facebook](https://www.fca.org.uk/news/blogs/better-outcomes-payment-firms-understand-customer-needs \"Share this selection on Facebook\")\n- [LinkedIn](https://www.fca.org.uk/news/blogs/better-outcomes-payment-firms-understand-customer-needs \"Share this selection on LinkedIn\")\n- [Share this selection by email](https://www.fca.org.uk/news/blogs/better-outcomes-payment-firms-understand-customer-needs \"Share this selection by email\")\n\nShare this selection\n\n- [Tweet](https://www.fca.org.uk/news/blogs/better-outcomes-payment-firms-understand-customer-needs \"Share this selection on Twitter\")\n- [Facebook](https://www.fca.org.uk/news/blogs/better-outcomes-payment-firms-understand-customer-needs \"Share this selection on Facebook\")\n- [LinkedIn](https://www.fca.org.uk/news/blogs/better-outcomes-payment-firms-understand-customer-needs \"Share this selection on LinkedIn\")\n- [Share this selection by email](https://www.fca.org.uk/news/blogs/better-outcomes-payment-firms-understand-customer-needs \"Share this selection by email\")\n\n- [Tweet](https://www.fca.org.uk/news/blogs/better-outcomes-payment-firms-understand-customer-needs \"Share this selection on Twitter\")\n- [Facebook](https://www.fca.org.uk/news/blogs/better-outcomes-payment-firms-understand-customer-needs \"Share this selection on Facebook\")\n- [LinkedIn](https://www.fca.org.uk/news/blogs/better-outcomes-payment-firms-understand-customer-needs \"Share this selection on LinkedIn\")\n- [Share this selection by email](https://www.fca.org.uk/news/blogs/better-outcomes-payment-firms-understand-customer-needs \"Share this selection by email\")\n\nShare this selection\n\n- [Tweet](https://www.fca.org.uk/news/blogs/better-outcomes-payment-firms-understand-customer-needs \"Share this selection on Twitter\")\n- [Facebook](https://www.fca.org.uk/news/blogs/better-outcomes-payment-firms-understand-customer-needs \"Share this selection on Facebook\")\n- [LinkedIn](https://www.fca.org.uk/news/blogs/better-outcomes-payment-firms-understand-customer-needs \"Share this selection on LinkedI